% Barman -- translated to InductorHTN syntax (reduced core).
%
% Source domain: the IPC "Barman" domain -- a robot bartender mixes cocktails
% from ingredients using shakers and shot glasses. The full domain has two
% hands, dispensers, and shot glasses; this core keeps one shaker and one hand
% so the file reads as a teaching example, not a benchmark.
%
% Pattern it teaches: RESOURCE / STATE TRACKING with a NUMERIC FLUENT, and
% TIGHT PRECONDITION ORDERING. Each pour increases a shaker fill level
% (increase numeric fluent); shaking is gated on the level reaching the recipe
% size, so the "mix" step can only fire once the resource is full.
%
% Idioms on show:
%   - increase() on a fact shaped pred(args, value) for resource counting
%   - gating a step on the fluent's value (shakeWhenFull checks the level) ->
%     an explicit ordering / readiness constraint
%   - clean/used state transition on the shaker (a consumable container)
%   - an operator combining a fluent effect and an add() in one body
%
% Note: the linter reports SEM002 "Undefined predicate: 2/0" on the numeric
% literal in the fluent gate. That is a known linter gap with integer literals
% in goal positions (the engine plans it fine); see exemplars/README.md.
%
% Run it:  goals(makeCocktail(cocktail1)).  -- gin + juice, shaken.

/* ---------- Operators ---------- */

% Pick the shaker up (the hand is the tracked resource).
opGraspShaker(?s) :- del(handEmpty), add(holding(?s)).

% Pour one ingredient into the shaker: +1 to the fill level.
opFill(?s, ?ing) :- increase(shakerLevel(?s), 1), add(contains(?s, ?ing)).

% Shake: consumes the shaker's "clean" state, yields the mixed cocktail.
opShake(?s, ?c) :- del(clean(?s)), add(used(?s), mixed(?s, ?c)).

/* ---------- Methods ---------- */

% makeCocktail(?c): grasp a clean empty shaker, pour both recipe ingredients,
% shake. The gate binds the two ingredients from the recipe (data), so the
% ordering of the pours is fixed by the recipe.
makeCocktail(?c) :-
    if(recipe(?c, ?ing1, ?ing2),
       shaker(?s), clean(?s), shakerLevel(?s, 0), handEmpty),
    do(opGraspShaker(?s),
       opFill(?s, ?ing1),
       opFill(?s, ?ing2),
       shakeWhenFull(?s, ?c)).

% Only shake once the shaker is full (both ingredients poured -> level 2). This
% is the fluent doing real work: it gates the mix step on the running count.
shakeWhenFull(?s, ?c) :- if(shakerLevel(?s, 2)), do(opShake(?s, ?c)).

/* ---------- World state ---------- */

% Types (unary facts -> linter sorts).
shaker(shaker1).
ingredient(gin).   ingredient(juice).
cocktail(cocktail1).

% Recipe (data, not methods).
recipe(cocktail1, gin, juice).

% Initial state: empty clean shaker, free hand.
handEmpty.
clean(shaker1).
shakerLevel(shaker1, 0).

goals(makeCocktail(cocktail1)).
